WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.580
Working muscle needs more oxygen.

2
00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.540
How does the blood know to let go of more there?

3
00:00:09.533 --> 00:00:14.773
Use the rule. Anything that binds one shape better shifts the mix toward that shape.

4
00:00:15.767 --> 00:00:21.827
Three regulators prefer T: acid carbon dioxide and a small molecule called BPG.

5
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.840
Its full name is 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate.

6
00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:30.000
It is a small anion with about four to five negative charges.

7
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:34.800
Red cells hold nearly one BPG for every hemoglobin.

8
00:00:35.883 --> 00:00:39.063
It wedges into a central pocket between the β chains.

9
00:00:39.943 --> 00:00:41.843
That pocket is wide open only in T.

10
00:00:42.833 --> 00:00:46.013
Predict. Add BPG to hemoglobin.

11
00:00:46.913 --> 00:00:48.333
Which way does P50 move?

12
00:00:56.207 --> 00:00:59.167
BPG holds the molecule in T, like a doorstop.

13
00:01:00.047 --> 00:01:03.387
It takes more oxygen to tip the mix, so the curve shifts right.

14
00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:06.150
Acid works through the Bohr effect.

15
00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:11.470
Protons add to certain groups, chiefly a histidine near the end of each β chain.

16
00:01:12.483 --> 00:01:18.043
Those groups can then form salt bridges that steady T. So low pH lowers oxygen

17
00:01:18.043 --> 00:01:19.043
affinity.

18
00:01:19.533 --> 00:01:21.193
Working muscle makes acid.

19
00:01:22.033 --> 00:01:27.153
The acid tips hemoglobin toward T exactly where a straining muscle needs the oxygen.

20
00:01:28.267 --> 00:01:32.707
Carbon dioxide binds the ends of hemoglobin's chains as carbamate.

21
00:01:33.527 --> 00:01:36.367
Those groups form their own salt bridges that favor T.

22
00:01:37.367 --> 00:01:43.287
In exercising muscle, three fast signals arrive at once: falling oxygen, falling pH,

23
00:01:43.567 --> 00:01:44.947
and rising carbon dioxide.

24
00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:48.340
Does a right shift spoil loading in the lungs?

25
00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:52.740
Acid and carbon dioxide are high in tissue, not in the lungs.

26
00:01:53.783 --> 00:01:56.183
So their shift happens where unloading happens.

27
00:01:57.233 --> 00:01:59.473
BPG shifts the curve everywhere.

28
00:02:00.193 --> 00:02:04.933
But the lung end sits on the flat top of the curve, so a modest shift costs little

29
00:02:04.933 --> 00:02:05.933
loading.

30
00:02:06.317 --> 00:02:10.657
A very large shift would start to cost loading, so more is not always better.

31
00:02:11.667 --> 00:02:13.507
BPG is the slower dial.

32
00:02:14.407 --> 00:02:18.707
It rises over hours to days at high altitude, part of how you acclimatize.

33
00:02:19.717 --> 00:02:24.037
Fetal hemoglobin has two γ chains in place of the two β chains.

34
00:02:24.817 --> 00:02:28.797
At position 143, it has a serine instead of a histidine.

35
00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:35.090
So it binds BPG weakly and stays at higher oxygen affinity than the mother's

36
00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:36.090
hemoglobin.

37
00:02:36.633 --> 00:02:41.173
That lets fetal blood pull oxygen across the placenta from the mother's blood.

38
00:02:42.167 --> 00:02:48.447
Acid, carbon dioxide and BPG all favor T. So they shift the mix toward T where

39
00:02:48.447 --> 00:02:49.567
oxygen must let go.
